# The words of the window of FreeSynergy Broadcast, English (ADR 0057: MessageFormat 2). Every id needs a German twin in de.mf2.
app-name = FreeSynergy Broadcast
app-tagline = Goal: Record once, send it to many places, and keep your stream key secret: one live stream goes on to several targets untouched, and is recorded.
soon = coming soon
state-connected = The server answers and speaks the contract {$contract}.
state-down-unreachable = No server is running.
state-down-timeout = A server holds the socket but does not answer within {$detail} ms.
state-down-protocol = The server answered something this window cannot read.
state-contract = This frontend does not fit the server: it speaks {$found}, this window needs {$expected} (same main level, minor level not lower).
state-hint-down = Start the program with the command fs-broadcast; it starts the server and this window.
state-hint-contract = Install a matching version of FreeSynergy Broadcast; the window below only shows what is planned.
today-title = What it can do today
today-1 = Opens this window, finds its server and checks that both speak the same contract version.
today-2 = Shows a first look at the main view, the dashboard (page "Broadcast"), with sample data, marked as a preview; nothing in it is live.
today-3 = The core (receive one RTMP stream, pass it untouched to many RTMP and RTMPS targets, record it as FLV, check a target before going live) exists as a library, tested only on the local machine, and is not connected to this window yet; the server only reports what it offers, which is nothing yet.
coming-title = What comes
coming-1 = Dashboard with real data: the input (address and a key that is shown once) and the targets with their state: live, error, disconnected.
coming-2 = Targets: add one (name, RTMP or RTMPS address, key hidden), switch it on or off, remove it.
coming-3 = Check a target before going live and show the result with the reason.
coming-4 = Input key: shown once, renewed on request; only a hash is stored, and the key never appears in a log or an error text.
coming-5 = Recording: start and stop, choose the folder with the file picker.
coming-6 = Status line: bitrate, duration, lost frames and the state of each target.
coming-7 = One target fails without disturbing the others: "Target X disconnected, reconnect".
coming-8 = A visible note that receiving is local and unencrypted by default; opening it to the network comes with a warning.
coming-9 = More, each planned, not scheduled, no promise: handing recordings to Files, a player page with an embed code, viewer numbers, a schedule with reminders in Calendars, connecting platforms by login, a joint chat, guests by link, other inputs (SRT, WebRTC), several qualities, automation through workflows (no macros of its own), and whether scenes and sources are wanted at all.
